@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Scopes (Reducer)") {
        Reducer counterpart of the classic Scopes tutorial. Splitting one growing `State`
        struct into a tree of `@Reducer` scopes, connected with `@SubState`/`@SuperState`,
        keeps each scope's `State` and `When` focused on one concern instead of one struct
        absorbing everything the screen needs to do.
    }

    @Section(title: "Designing Scopes") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll read a feature already split into three `@Reducer` scopes — NewsFeed
            (feature toggles), NewsFeedList (article list + favorites), and NewsFeedArticle
            (article detail) — and see how `@SubState` wires them together.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Shared dependencies and DTOs, injected into whichever scope needs them via
                `ReducerDependencies` — the same `Injectable` types used by the classic pattern.

                @Code(name: "Dependencies.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-Dependencies-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                The root scope, `NewsFeedReducer`. `@SubState var atList: NewsFeedListReducer.State?`
                is the entire declaration of "this scope owns a NewsFeedList child" — no separate
                wiring step, no property wrapper on a class.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeed-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `update()` creates the child by assigning its `State`. `favoritesEnabled` flows
                down as a plain constructor parameter — no injection needed for data this local
                to one parent/child relationship.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeed-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedListReducer` owns the article list — and, via its own `@SubState var
                readingArticle`, a grandchild of the root scope.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedList-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `.navigateFromListToChild` creates `readingArticle` the same way the root created
                `atList` — copying `favoritesEnabled` down again. `.favorite` resolves two injected
                dependencies (`DateProvider`, `PersistenceProvider`) to persist the change.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedList-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedArticleReducer` — the leaf of the tree. It has no children of its own,
                so no `@SubState` here.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedArticleReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedArticle-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Its `.favorite` handling is a near-duplicate of the list's — the same tradeoff the
                classic tutorial calls out: splitting scopes trades a "god object" for some
                duplicated logic across siblings. Extracting a shared helper function is the usual
                fix; Statoscope doesn't need anything more elaborate than that.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedArticleReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedArticle-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Sending an event to the root creates the child scope synchronously — the child
                `Store` exists the moment `update()` returns, no separate wait step.

                @Code(name: "ScopesTests.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-Tests-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                The same holds two levels down: sending to the list scope creates the grandchild,
                and its state is reachable immediately at `scope.state.readingArticle`.

                @Code(name: "ScopesTests.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-Tests-02.swift)
            }

            One thing worth naming explicitly: nothing here *requires* three scopes. A single
            `NewsFeedReducer` with a bigger `State` struct would work too. Split when a piece of
            state and its `When` cases genuinely belong to a narrower concern than the whole
            screen — not by default.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Views and Scopes") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Each scope in the tree gets its own `StoreViewProtocol` view. Statoscope resolves
            the parent `Store` from the SwiftUI environment and connects each child automatically.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                The root view switches on `loadingFeatureToggles`, then hands off to
                `AutoConnectedView` once the `atList` child exists — it reads the child `Store`
                from the environment `NewsFeedReducer.Store` injected, and renders nothing until
                the child is actually there.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedRootView.swift", file: NewsFeedRootView-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                For the list-to-detail push, a hand-built `Binding` derived from
                `readingArticle != nil` works for the read side — but clearing it back to `nil`
                needs a dismiss `When` case wired up by hand.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListView.swift", file: NewsFeedListView-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedListReducer.bind(_:in:dismissWhen:send:)` replaces the hand-built
                `Binding`, and the macro-generated `buildReadingArticleView(content:)` —
                generated from the `@SubState var readingArticle` declared a few steps ago —
                replaces the manual environment lookup for the child store. Pair it with
                whichever presentation modifier fits — `.navigationDestination` here, or
                `.sheet`/`.fullScreenCover`/`NavigationLink` elsewhere — it's safe as content
                for any of them, at any nesting depth.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListView.swift", file: NewsFeedListView-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                The leaf view: no children, no navigation, just `model`/`send` like any other
                `StoreViewProtocol`.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedArticleView.swift", file: NewsFeedArticleView-01.swift)
            }

            Every scope in the tree — root, list, article — is independently testable via
            `GIVEN`/`WHEN`/`THEN` and independently viewable, because each one only ever knows
            its own `State` and `When`.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Reacting to child events: MiddlewareReducer") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Back in "Designing Scopes," `NewsFeedListReducer` and `NewsFeedArticleReducer` each
            kept their own copy of `favorites` and duplicated the toggle-and-persist logic in
            `.favorite(id:)` — favoriting an article from the detail view had no way to update
            the list's own copy. `@SubState`/`@SuperState` only move data *down* the tree (parent
            creates child) or give a *read-only* snapshot of an ancestor. Neither lets a child's
            event change what a parent owns. `MiddlewareReducer` is the piece that does: an
            ancestor reacts to an event *after* the child that sent it has already processed it.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Move `favorites` up to the root and make it the single owner. `NewsFeedReducer`
                conforms to `MiddlewareReducer` and implements `updateSubstate`, which runs
                **after** the event has already reached whichever child sent it — `childState`
                is that child's state once its own `update()` finished. Matching either
                `NewsFeedListReducer.When.favorite` or `NewsFeedArticleReducer.When.favorite` and
                returning `.toggleFavorite(id:)` sends that `When` to the root's own `update()`.
                The event was already forwarded to the child by the time this runs — there's no
                separate step to opt into that, and no way to prevent it.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeed-03.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `updateSubstate` returns a plain `When?` — `nil` for no reaction, or a `When` to
                send to the parent's own `update()`. This mirrors `statoscope-zustand`'s
                `onChildAction(action, childState)`, which documents the same ordering choice:
                the child's own state passed in is already updated by the time an ancestor sees
                the event.

                That ordering is exactly why a parent reaction can safely destroy or replace the
                child subtree the event came from — say, tearing the child down after three
                failed attempts — without any risk of the event still being "in flight" toward a
                subtree that no longer exists. The child already, safely, applied it to itself
                first. A real app hit the *opposite* problem under an earlier design where the
                parent reacted **before** forwarding: a login screen's own `.userSubmitsForm`
                handler guarded on `!state.submitting`, where `submitting` read a parent field
                that the parent's reaction to that *same* event had already flipped to `true`
                moments earlier — rejecting every legitimate submit. Forwarding first, always,
                is what makes that entire class of bug impossible rather than just avoidable.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeed-03.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedListReducer` drops its local `favorites` field and the toggle/persist
                logic entirely — `.favorite` still runs in its own `update()` (forwarding always
                happens), it's just a no-op now — and reads the shared list via `@SuperState`
                instead. Notice `NewsFeedListReducer` implements no `MiddlewareReducer` of its
                own: the root still reacts to events from *its* child, `NewsFeedArticleReducer`,
                two levels down, with no relay code needed here. Every ancestor's
                `updateSubstate` is checked for every descendant's event, regardless of depth —
                an intermediate reducer that doesn't care about an event simply doesn't match it.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedList-03.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedArticleReducer` gets the same treatment, and its `@SuperState` skips
                its direct parent (`NewsFeedListReducer`) to read the root two levels up. That's
                safe here specifically because `favorites` is declared directly on
                `NewsFeedReducer`'s own `State` — an ancestor's own state is always committed
                immediately after its own `update()` runs, so a snapshot of it is never stale.
                It would **not** be safe to skip a level to read a field that itself only mirrors
                some *other*, independently-updated descendant's data — that mirrored copy only
                refreshes when the reducer holding it processes an event of its own, so it can
                go stale exactly when you need it most. Always point `@SuperState` at whichever
                reducer actually **owns** the data, not one that merely relays it.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedArticleReducer.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-NewsFeedArticle-03.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                The view follows the same change: `model.favorites` becomes
                `model.newsFeed.favorites`, read live off the root through the new
                `@SuperState`. It can no longer disagree with the list, because there's only
                one `favorites` left to disagree with.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedArticleView.swift", file: NewsFeedArticleView-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Proof it works: sending `.favorite(id:)` to `readingArticle` — three levels
                below the root, through `atList` — still lands in the root's own `favorites`,
                with no code at all in `NewsFeedListReducer` to make that happen.

                @Code(name: "ScopesTests.swift", file: Scopes-Reducer-Tests-03.swift)
            }

            Three different tools now cover three different directions of data flow in a scope
            tree: a plain constructor parameter for a value fixed at creation time (like
            `favoritesEnabled` in "Designing Scopes"), `@SuperState` for a child reading an
            ancestor's *current* value, and `MiddlewareReducer` for a child's event changing
            what an ancestor owns. Reach for the last one whenever two reducers need to agree on
            something neither of them should have to own twice.
        }
    }
}
